 \section SADeprecated Deprecation Notice

 \deprecated This plug-in is deprecated and will be removed in Helios v2.0. Bounding boxes, segmentation masks and depth images can now be written directly by the \ref VisualizerDoc "Visualizer" plug-in (see \ref Annotation), using the same labeling convention and file formats as the camera annotations of the radiation plug-in. New code should use the %Visualizer; this plug-in continues to work unchanged until it is removed.

 The table below lists the %Visualizer equivalent of each part of this plug-in.

 <table>
 <tr><th>This plug-in</th><th>%Visualizer equivalent</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::labelPrimitives( const std::vector<std::vector<uint>>&, const char* ) "labelPrimitives()" with one group of UUIDs per object</td><td>Set primitive data named after the class on each object's primitives, with a different <code>uint</code> value for each object: <code>context.setPrimitiveData( UUIDs, "fruit", objectID )</code>.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableObjectDetection() "Object detection" (bounding boxes)</td><td>Visualizer::writeImageBoundingBoxes(), or Visualizer::writeImageBoundingBoxes_ObjectData() for labels stored as object data.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableInstanceSegmentation() "Instance segmentation" (COCO masks)</td><td>Visualizer::writeImageSegmentationMasks(), or Visualizer::writeImageSegmentationMasks_ObjectData().</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableSemanticSegmentation() "Semantic segmentation" and <code>pixelID_combined.txt</code></td><td>No direct equivalent. Visualizer::getPixelUUIDs() returns the primitive visible in each pixel, from which a per-pixel class image can be built.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>RGB rendering</td><td>Visualizer::printWindow().</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::setCameraPosition( const std::vector<helios::vec3>&, const std::vector<helios::vec3>& ) "Multiple camera views"</td><td>Loop over the views, calling Visualizer::setCameraPosition() and then writing the image and annotations for each.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::setMinimumLabelPixels() "Minimum label pixels"</td><td>No equivalent: there is no threshold to set. As for the radiation plug-in, every visible labeled object has a bounding box, and only a region of one or two pixels has no mask.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>(none)</td><td>Depth images: Visualizer::writeDepthImageData(), Visualizer::writeDepthImageDataEXR() and Visualizer::writeNormDepthImage().</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section SAissues Known Issues

 - The RGB rendering is drawn to a window, so the plug-in requires a display. It cannot be run on a headless machine. (The annotation passes themselves render offscreen and would not need one.)
 - Object detection bounding boxes are computed from the visible (occluded) extent of each object. An object split into several disconnected visible regions by an occluder is still described by a single box spanning all of them.

 \section SAIntro Introduction

 Training a machine learning model for a computer vision task requires a large number of labeled images. Producing those labels by hand is slow and error-prone, and for a plant canopy it is often ambiguous where one leaf ends and the next begins.

 This plug-in generates labeled images automatically. It renders a scene that already exists in the Helios context, and because it knows exactly which primitives belong to which object, it can write out the corresponding annotations without any manual work. Three annotation types are supported:

 - <b>Object detection</b>: a rectangular bounding box around each object, in the normalized format used by YOLO.
 - <b>Semantic segmentation</b>: a single image-sized mask giving the class of each pixel.
 - <b>Instance segmentation</b>: a separate mask per object, giving the un-occluded extent of that object.

 \section SAConstructor SyntheticAnnotation Class Constructor

 <table>
 <tr><th>Constructors
 <tr><td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::SyntheticAnnotation( helios::Context* context) "SyntheticAnnotation( helios::Context* context)"
 </table>

 The \ref SyntheticAnnotation class is initialized by passing a pointer to the Helios context.

 \section SAUsage Using the Plug-in

 \subsection SALabeling Labeling Primitives

 Before anything can be rendered, the primitives making up each object must be assigned a label. A <b>label</b> is a class name shared by many objects (for example "leaf"), and an <b>object</b> is one instance of that class (one particular leaf). The \ref SyntheticAnnotation::labelPrimitives(const std::vector<std::vector<uint>> &UUIDs, const char *label) "labelPrimitives()" method takes the UUIDs of the primitives and the label to assign them.

 The nesting of the UUID vector determines how objects are formed:

 <table>
 <tr><th>Call <th>Result
 <tr><td><code>labelPrimitives( UUID, "leaf" )</code> <td>The single primitive becomes one object with label "leaf".
 <tr><td><code>labelPrimitives( UUIDs, "leaf" )</code> <td>All primitives in the vector become <b>one</b> object with label "leaf".
 <tr><td><code>labelPrimitives( UUID_groups, "leaf" )</code> <td>Each inner vector becomes a <b>separate</b> object, all with label "leaf".
 <tr><td><code>labelPrimitives( "leaf" )</code> <td>Every primitive in the context becomes one object with label "leaf".
 </table>

 The nested form is the one normally wanted, because a plant canopy has many leaves that should be separate objects of the same class. Plug-ins that generate geometry typically return UUIDs already grouped this way.

 This example additionally requires the \ref CanopyGeneratorDoc "CanopyGenerator" plug-in, so the project's CMakeLists.txt would use <code>set( PLUGINS "syntheticannotation;canopygenerator" )</code>.

 \code{.cpp}
 #include "SyntheticAnnotation.h"
 #include "CanopyGenerator.h"

 int main() {

     helios::Context context;

     CanopyGenerator canopygenerator(&context);
     canopygenerator.buildCanopy( VSPGrapevineParameters() );

     SyntheticAnnotation annotation(&context);

     // getLeafUUIDs(PlantID) returns one inner vector per leaf, which is exactly the nesting
     // labelPrimitives() expects, so each leaf becomes a separate object
     annotation.labelPrimitives( canopygenerator.getLeafUUIDs(0), "leaves" );

     // getFruitUUIDs(PlantID) is nested one level deeper (cluster, then fruit, then primitives),
     // so the cluster level is flattened away to make each individual berry an object
     std::vector<std::vector<uint>> berries;
     for( const std::vector<std::vector<uint>> &cluster : canopygenerator.getFruitUUIDs(0) ){
         for( const std::vector<uint> &berry : cluster ){
             berries.push_back( berry );
         }
     }
     annotation.labelPrimitives( berries, "berries" );

     annotation.render( "./annotations/" );

 }
 \endcode

 \ref SyntheticAnnotation::labelUnlabeledPrimitives(const char *label) "labelUnlabeledPrimitives()" assigns a label to every primitive that is not already labeled, placing each in its own object. It should be called after all other labeling calls, since it acts on whatever remains unlabeled at that moment.

 Primitives that are never labeled are not ignored: they are rendered in white in the ID image, and so they occlude labeled objects behind them exactly as they should.

 \subsection SACamera Setting the Camera

 \ref SyntheticAnnotation::setCameraPosition(const std::vector<helios::vec3> &camera_position, const std::vector<helios::vec3> &camera_lookat) "setCameraPosition()" takes a camera position and a look-at point. Passing vectors of each instead of single values renders the scene from several viewpoints in one call, which is the usual way to generate a dataset from a single scene. The image resolution is set with \ref SyntheticAnnotation::setWindowSize(uint window_width, uint window_height) "setWindowSize()".

 \code{.cpp}
 std::vector<helios::vec3> camera_positions;
 std::vector<helios::vec3> camera_lookats;
 for( int i=0; i<10; i++ ){
     camera_positions.push_back( helios::make_vec3(0.5f*float(i), -2, 1) );
     camera_lookats.push_back( helios::make_vec3(0.5f*float(i), 0, 1) );
 }
 annotation.setCameraPosition( camera_positions, camera_lookats );
 annotation.setWindowSize( 1024, 768 );
 \endcode

 These settings can also come from a loaded XML file, via the global data <code>camera_position</code> (vec3), <code>camera_lookat</code> (vec3), and <code>image_resolution</code> (int2). Values found in the XML override those set through the API. The global data <code>object_detection</code>, <code>semantic_segmentation</code>, and <code>instance_segmentation</code> take the string value <code>"enabled"</code> or <code>"disabled"</code> to control which annotation types are written; any other string value is an error. Global data of the right name but the wrong type produces a warning and is ignored, leaving the value set through the API in place.

 \subsection SAOutputs Selecting Which Annotations to Write

 All three annotation types are enabled by default. Each can be turned on or off independently:

 <table>
 <tr><th>Annotation Type <th>Enable <th>Disable
 <tr><td>Object detection (bounding boxes) <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableObjectDetection() "enableObjectDetection()" <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::disableObjectDetection() "disableObjectDetection()"
 <tr><td>Semantic segmentation <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableSemanticSegmentation() "enableSemanticSegmentation()" <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::disableSemanticSegmentation() "disableSemanticSegmentation()"
 <tr><td>Instance segmentation <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableInstanceSegmentation() "enableInstanceSegmentation()" <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::disableInstanceSegmentation() "disableInstanceSegmentation()"
 </table>

 Instance segmentation renders each object separately, so its cost grows with the number of objects. Disabling it is worthwhile when only bounding boxes are needed.

 \ref SyntheticAnnotation::setMinimumLabelPixels(int labelminpixels) "setMinimumLabelPixels()" sets how many pixels an object must cover to be written out at all; the default is 10. Raising it excludes objects that are almost entirely occluded and would otherwise contribute near-empty labels to the training data.

 The appearance of the RGB rendering is controlled with \ref SyntheticAnnotation::setBackgroundColor(const helios::RGBcolor &color) "setBackgroundColor()" and \ref SyntheticAnnotation::addSkyDome(const char *filename) "addSkyDome()", the latter taking the path to a sky texture image.

 \subsection SARender Generating the Images

 \ref SyntheticAnnotation::render(const char *outputdir) "render()" performs the rendering and writes every enabled annotation. Its argument is the base output directory, which is created if it does not exist.

 \code{.cpp}
 annotation.render( "./annotations/" );
 \endcode

