 <table>
 <tr><th>Setting <th>Default <th>Set with
 <tr><td>Image resolution <td>1000 &times; 800 <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::setWindowSize(uint window_width, uint window_height) "setWindowSize()"
 <tr><td>Camera position / look-at <td>(1, 0, 1) looking at (0, 0, 1) <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::setCameraPosition(const std::vector<helios::vec3> &camera_position, const std::vector<helios::vec3> &camera_lookat) "setCameraPosition()"
 <tr><td>Background color <td>(0.9, 0.9, 0.9) <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::setBackgroundColor(const helios::RGBcolor &color) "setBackgroundColor()"
 <tr><td>Sky dome texture <td><code>plugins/visualizer/textures/SkyDome_clouds.jpg</code> <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::addSkyDome(const char *filename) "addSkyDome()"
 <tr><td>Minimum label pixels <td>10 <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::setMinimumLabelPixels(int labelminpixels) "setMinimumLabelPixels()"
 <tr><td>Object detection <td>Enabled <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableObjectDetection() "enableObjectDetection()"
 <tr><td>Semantic segmentation <td>Enabled <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableSemanticSegmentation() "enableSemanticSegmentation()"
 <tr><td>Instance segmentation <td>Enabled <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::enableInstanceSegmentation() "enableInstanceSegmentation()"
 <tr><td>Progress messages <td>Enabled <td>\ref SyntheticAnnotation::disableMessages() "disableMessages()"
 </table>

 \section SAFiles Output File Structure

 Output is organized with one sub-directory per camera view:

 \code
 annotations/
 ├── ID_mapping.txt
 ├── view00000/
 │   ├── RGB_rendering.jpeg
 │   ├── RGB_rendering.txt
 │   ├── classes.txt
 │   ├── instances.json
 │   ├── pixelID_combined.txt
 │   ├── semantic_segmentation.txt
 │   └── semantic_segmentation_ID_mapping.txt
 └── view00001/
     └── ...
 \endcode

 \warning Two different class numbering schemes appear in these files. <code>classes.txt</code>, <code>ID_mapping.txt</code> and the bounding-box files use <b>zero-based</b> class indices. The semantic segmentation mask instead uses <b>one-based</b> indices, described by its own <code>semantic_segmentation_ID_mapping.txt</code>. A label that is class 0 in the bounding boxes is therefore class 1 in the semantic mask.

 <table>
 <tr><th>File <th>Description
 <tr><td><code>ID_mapping.txt</code> <td>A header line <code>object_ID label class_ID</code> followed by one row per object giving its integer object ID, its label, and its zero-based class index. This is what allows the integer IDs appearing in <code>pixelID_combined.txt</code> to be traced back to a label.
 <tr><td><code>RGB_rendering.jpeg</code> <td>The rendered color image, which is the image the annotations describe.
 <tr><td><code>RGB_rendering.txt</code> <td>Bounding boxes for every object visible in the view, one per line, in the YOLO annotation format: <code>class_ID x_center y_center width height</code>. The four geometric values are fractions of the image dimensions, with the origin at the top-left. The file is named after the image so that a training pipeline can pair the two automatically.
 <tr><td><code>classes.txt</code> <td>Class names, one per line, in class-index order and with no header. It sits beside the bounding-box file because that is where a reader looks for it when no class file is named explicitly.
 <tr><td><code>instances.json</code> <td>Instance segmentation masks in COCO format, with one annotation per visible object. Mask outlines are given in absolute pixels with the origin at the top-left.
 <tr><td><code>pixelID_combined.txt</code> <td>Object ID of each pixel, as a whitespace-separated grid of integers with one row per image row, ordered top-down to match the rendered image. Unlabeled pixels and background carry the ID code of white, 16777215.
 <tr><td><code>semantic_segmentation.txt</code> <td>A single multi-class mask covering all labels, ordered top-down: one integer per pixel giving the one-based class of that pixel. Background and unlabeled pixels carry 16777215.
 <tr><td><code>semantic_segmentation_ID_mapping.txt</code> <td>A header line <code>label class_ID</code> followed by one row per label giving its name and its one-based class index in the semantic mask.
 </table>

 \section SAVisualize Viewing the Annotations

 The bounding boxes and the instance masks are written in the formats the \ref VisualizerDoc "Visualizer" reads, so they can be displayed over the rendered image without any conversion:

 \code{.cpp}
 Visualizer visualizer(800, 600);

 // Bounding boxes. The class names are picked up from classes.txt beside the annotation file.
 visualizer.displayImageWithBoundingBoxes("./annotations/view00000/RGB_rendering.jpeg", "./annotations/view00000/RGB_rendering.txt");

 // Instance segmentation masks.
 visualizer.displayImageWithSegmentationMasks("./annotations/view00000/RGB_rendering.jpeg", "./annotations/view00000/instances.json");
 \endcode

 These are the same formats the \ref RadiationDoc "Radiation" plug-in writes for its cameras, so annotations from the two plug-ins can be used interchangeably.

 \section SAOcclusion Occlusion

 Every annotation describes what is actually <b>visible</b> in the image. All of them are computed from a single rendering of the whole scene, so a leaf half-hidden behind a stem contributes only its visible half to its bounding box, its instance mask, and the semantic mask alike.

 This is the convention the COCO and YOLO formats assume, and it matches what the \ref RadiationDoc "Radiation" plug-in produces for its cameras. An object hidden entirely behind another is not annotated at all, and one reduced to a sliver too small to be useful is dropped by the minimum-pixel threshold (see \ref SyntheticAnnotation::setMinimumLabelPixels(int labelminpixels) "setMinimumLabelPixels()").

 \note Earlier versions wrote instance masks giving each object's full <b>un-occluded</b> extent, by rendering every object separately. Those per-object mask files are no longer produced.

 \section SAImplementation How It Works

 Understanding the mechanism explains several of the plug-in's constraints.

 Each object is assigned an integer ID, which is encoded as an RGB color by splitting the integer across the three color channels. Every primitive belonging to that object is temporarily recolored with that code, the scene is rendered, and the ID of each pixel is recovered by reading the rendered image back and decoding the color. The primitives' original colors are restored before \ref SyntheticAnnotation::render(const char *outputdir) "render()" returns, including if an error occurs partway through.

 This means the rendered image is being used to carry data rather than to be looked at, so the ID pass is rendered with anti-aliasing disabled, with no lighting, and with the \ref VisualizerDoc "Visualizer" in exact-color mode (\ref Visualizer::enableExactColorMode()). Anti-aliasing would blend the ID colors of adjacent objects into values that decode to unrelated IDs, and any shading or brightening of the color would corrupt the encoded integer.

 The RGB rendering is a completely separate pass, using its own \ref VisualizerDoc "Visualizer" configured the opposite way -- anti-aliasing enabled and Phong lighting with shadows -- because that image is meant to be looked at. That pass draws to a window, which is why the plug-in requires a display.

 Every annotation is derived from that one ID rendering per view, so adding more objects to a scene does not add more rendering passes.

*/
